<title>JAPAN: BOJ &quot;tankan&quot; shows Japan firms see more spending.</title>
<headline>BOJ &quot;tankan&quot; shows Japan firms see more spending.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1997-06-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Major Japanese companies plan a 4.6 percent increase in capital investment in fiscal 1997/98, following a 4.0 percent rise the previous year, the Bank of Japan (BOJ) said in its June &quot;tankan&quot; short-term corporate survey released on Wednesday.  </p>
<p>Forecasts for capital spending in 1997/98:</p>
<p>					 1997/98     1997/98     1996/97</p>
<p>					 latest	previous    result</p>
<p>					 forecast    forecast</p>
<p> Major firms			  +4.6	  +2.7	  +4.0</p>
<p>    Manufacturers		   +8.5	  +2.7	  +5.7</p>
<p>    Non-manufacturers	     +2.7	  +2.7	  +3.2</p>
<p> Small firms			 -12.6	 -18.0	  +3.5</p>
<p>    Manufacturers		   -2.2	 -10.0	  +2.2</p>
<p>    Non-manufacturers	    -17.1	 -21.4	  +4.1</p>
<p> Financial (200 firms)	    +7.7	  +5.3	 -11.2</p>
<p>    Banking (148 firms)	   +6.3	  +5.6	  -3.6</p>
<p>    Securities (27 firms)	+46.6	 +36.7	  +5.4</p>
<p>Forecast for corporate sales in 1997/98</p>
<p>    Major manufacturers	   +3.1	  +2.6	  +6.5</p>
<p>    Major non-manufacturers     +2.3	  +2.2	  +4.1</p>
<p>Forecast for current profits in 1997/98</p>
<p>    Major manufacturers	   +8.5	  +9.4	 +20.0</p>
<p>(Figure corrected to +8.5 percent in preceding alert. Figures exclude oil refineries)</p>
<p>    Major non-manufacturers     +4.0	  +4.6	  +9.8</p>
<p>(Figure corrected to +4.0 in preceding alert. Figures exclude electric power and gas firms)</p>
<p>Note: The tankan covered 9,469 companies, of which 708 firms were large. Large manufacturers surveyed used an average dollar/yen rate of 110.29 yen when they made sales and profit forecasts for the 1996/97 fiscal year. They used 109.00 yen in the previous survey conducted in March. For 1997/98 forecasts the manufacturers used an average dollar/yen rate of 114.56 yen to the dollar.</p>
